# Strategy Trade-Path Visualization

- Do not generate or refresh an interactive trade-path HTML for ordinary diagnostics by default. For routine research, backtests, audits, leverage checks, phase checks, and parameter probes, prefer machine JSON plus a Markdown diagnostic unless the user explicitly asks for a chart/path/HTML/visualization.
- Generate or refresh the chart only when the user explicitly asks, or when a strategy version is formally registered/frozen as `Vx`.
- Material changes to entry, exit, reversal, protection, or sizing behavior require a chart only if they are part of formal version registration/freeze, or the user explicitly asks to inspect the path.
- Default to the registered/candidate base leverage and primary execution phase. Leveraged or phase variants get separate charts only when the user explicitly asks for that variant's path.
- Save a self-contained interactive HTML under the family `artifacts/` directory and keep its reproducible renderer under `scripts/`. Do not use Cursor Canvas.
- The chart must include the full price history used by the frozen backtest, candlesticks, the primary signal/indicator, equity, entry and exit markers, and a trade table.
- Connect each trade's entry and corresponding exit with one visible line. Use distinct long/short colors; label forced reversals so an exit and the new opposite-side entry can be audited at the shared timestamp.
- Support zoom, pan, hover details, and click-to-focus from the trade table when practical. The HTML must not depend on a local dev server or external CDN.
- Build the chart from the same retained backtest result and frozen data window as the reported metrics; do not reconstruct trades from prose.
- Before delivery, verify: output exists; embedded payload is complete; chart trade count equals `closed_trades`; trade IDs are unique; every `entry_ts <= exit_ts`; every trade has both endpoints and enters the line-rendering path; no template placeholder remains.
- Update the family artifact index and core-ledger evidence map with relative Markdown links.
- In the final chat reply, use the repository-relative path as link text and the file's absolute POSIX path as href, then preflight the exact file with Cursor `open_resource` when available. Never use `../` or `../../` in a chat href.
